  "raw_text": "Step 1: Consider the polynomial \\(P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\\). We know that \\(|P(x)| \\leq 1\\) for all \\(x\\) in the interval \\([0, 1]\\).\n\nStep 2: Evaluate \\(P(x)\\) at the endpoints and midpoint of the interval \\([0, 1]\\):\n- At \\(x = 0\\), \\(P(0) = c\\).\n- At \\(x = 1\\), \\(P(1) = a + b + c\\).\n- At \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(P\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c\\).\n\nStep 3: Since \\(|P(x)| \\leq 1\\) for all \\(x\\) in \\([0, 1]\\), we have:\n- \\(|c| \\leq 1\\),\n- \\(|a + b + c| \\leq 1\\),\n- \\(\\left|\\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c\\right| \\leq 1\\).\n\nStep 4: To maximize \\(|a| + |b| + |c|\\), consider the case where \\(a, b, c\\) take values that satisfy the above inequalities and try to find the maximum possible sum of their absolute values.\n\nStep 5: Assume \\(c = 1\\). Then \\(|a + b + 1| \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\left|\\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + 1\\right| \\leq 1\\). Simplify these inequalities:\n- From \\(|a + b + 1| \\leq 1\\), we get \\(-2 \\leq a + b \\leq 0\\).\n- From \\(\\left|\\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + 1\\right| \\leq 1\\), we get \\(-2 \\leq \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} \\leq 0\\), or equivalently, \\(-8 \\leq a + 2b \\leq 0\\).\n\nStep 6: Solve the system of inequalities:\n- \\( -2 \\leq a + b \\leq 0 \\)\n- \\( -8 \\leq a + 2b \\leq 0 \\)\n\nStep 7: Subtract the first inequality from the second:\n- \\(-6 \\leq b \\leq 2\\).\n\nStep 8: Substitute \\(b = -2\\) into \\(a + b \\leq 0\\):\n- \\(a - 2 \\leq 0 \\Rightarrow a \\leq 2\\).\n\nStep 9: Substitute \\(b = -2\\) into \\(a + b \\geq -2\\):\n- \\(a - 2 \\geq -2 \\Rightarrow a \\geq 0\\).\n\nStep 10: Therefore, \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = -2\\) satisfy both inequalities. Check if this satisfies the original conditions:\n- \\(P(0) = 1\\),\n- \\(P(1) = 2 - 2 + 1 = 1\\),\n- \\(P\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{2}{4} - \\frac{2}{2} + 1 = \\frac{1}{2} - 1 + 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nAll conditions are satisfied. Thus, \\(|a| + |b| + |c| = 2 + 2 